# Why averages make a bell
subtitle: "Why the averages of lopsided data, like hospital stays, pile up into a bell — and why doubling the sample doesn't halve the error."

## Why averages make a bell
use: sampling_dist
with:
    shape: skewed
    'n': 4
    box:
      x: 40
      'y': 150
      w: 920
      h: 530

    shape p1 #b5482e shape circle x 239.6 y 493.7 radius 8 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    shape p2 #b5482e shape circle x 303.2 y 493.7 radius 8 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    shape p3 #b5482e shape circle x 366.8 y 493.7 radius 8 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    shape p6 #b5482e shape circle x 557.7 y 493.7 radius 8 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    group four members ["p1","p2","p3","p6"] hidden true
    shape avg #b5482e shape circle x 366.8 y 205.2 radius 8 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    label mean_lbl "2.7 days" above-right attach_to pop_mean hidden true
    text stays "Hospital stays" x 640 y 600 font_size 26 hidden true
    text days1 "days" x 905 y 470 font_size 22 hidden true
    text days2 "days" x 905 y 182 font_size 22 hidden true
    text nl4 "averages of 4" #b5482e x 770 y 380 font_size 26 hidden true
    text nl8 "averages of 8" #b5482e x 770 y 380 font_size 26 hidden true
    text se4 "± 0.93 days" #b5482e x 565 y 245 font_size 28 hidden true
    text se8 "± 0.65 days" #b5482e x 565 y 245 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Why averages make a bell"

    hide n_label se_label

Why do averages come out bell-shaped when the data isn't? Take hospital stays on a short-stay ward, in made-up numbers: most patients leave within two or three days, a few stay a week or more.
    draw axes pop_curve stays days1 days2

In this example the average stay is 2.7 days, pulled to the right of the peak by that long tail.
    draw pop_mean mean_lbl

Now pick four patients at random. Say they stayed one, two, three and six days.
    draw four parallel true

Their average, three days, becomes one dot on the lower line.
    draw avg

Repeat that thousands of times, and the dots pile up into this: averages of four. Already close to a bell, centred on the same 2.7.
    draw samp_curve samp_mean nl4

    erase four avg

The tail can't survive averaging.
    point at.x 653.1 at.y 497.7 gesture circle

An average of seven days needs all four patients to be long stays at once, which almost never happens.
    point at.x 624 at.y 212 gesture circle

An average near 2.7 can come from countless mixes of short and long stays. More ways in, a taller middle.
    point at.x 347.7 at.y 307.2 gesture tap

The bracket is the typical miss of an average of four: just under a day either way.
    draw se_bracket se4

    erase se4 nl4

Average eight patients instead, twice as many, and the bell narrows: the typical miss falls to about two thirds of a day.
    animate n 8 duration 4

    draw se8 nl8 parallel true

Twice the patients, yet the miss shrank by under a third, not by half: precision grows only with the square root of the sample.
    highlight se8

The patients themselves stay as lopsided as ever. The bell isn't in the data; it's in the averaging.
    highlight pop_curve

That is the central limit theorem, and it holds for almost any data with a finite spread. George Pólya named it in 1920: central to probability.
    point at.x 347.7 at.y 307.2 gesture circle

    quiz "Averages of four patients miss by about 0.9 days. How many patients halve that miss?"
        right: Sixteen: the miss shrinks with the square root of the sample, so halving it takes four times as many patients.
        * 8
        + 16
        * 6
---
